What is data?
Data" is simply information — raw facts, figures, or values that can be stored, processed, and used to gain understanding or make decisions.

In everyday terms
Data is anything that represents a fact about the world:
•	Your name: "Rohan Gupta"
•	Your age: 25
•	Today's date: 2026-08-10
•	A temperature reading: 32.5°C

Database Type -------------------------------------------------------------
1. Relational (SQL) Databases
Data stored in tables with rows and columns, connected via keys. Follows a strict schema.
•	Examples: MySQL, PostgreSQL, Oracle, SQL Server, SQLite

SQL example based on a real-world project/scenario,
e-commerce order management system.

==data interchange/markup formats, each suited to different jobs.


2. NoSQL Databases — these split into several sub-types:
•	Document databases — store data as JSON-like documents, flexible schema 
o	Examples: MongoDB, CouchDB
•	Key-Value stores — simplest model, data stored as key-value pairs 
o	Examples: Redis, DynamoDB
•	Column-family (wide-column) stores — optimized for large-scale, sparse data 
o	Examples: Cassandra, HBase
•	Graph databases — data stored as nodes and relationships, ideal for connected data (social networks, recommendations) 
o	Examples: Neo4j, ArangoDB
What is SQL?
SQL = Structured Query Language
SQL is used to create, store, retrieve, update, and delete data in relational databases such as MySQL, PostgreSQL, Oracle, and SQL Server.

MySQL was originally developed by Michael Widenius, David Axmark, and Allan Larsson in the 1990s, released by MySQL AB, acquired by Sun Microsystems in 2008, and became part of Oracle after Oracle acquired Sun in 2010.

1. Download MySQL
Go to the official MySQL website:
Download MySQL Installer for Windows
Choose MySQL Installer for Windows.
You will usually see two options:
•	Web Installer — smaller download; downloads components during installation. 
•	Full Installer — larger download; useful if you want to install without downloading everything again. 
For most users, choose Web Installer.
2. Start the installation
Run the downloaded .msi file.
When the installer asks for setup type, select:
Developer Default
This is recommended if you're learning/developing with MySQL because it installs:
•	MySQL Server 
•	MySQL Workbench 
•	MySQL Shell 
•	MySQL connectors and development tools 
Then click Next.
3. Install the required components
The installer will check for prerequisites.
Click:
Execute → Next
Wait for all components to install.
4. Configure MySQL Server
When you reach Type and Networking:
•	Configuration Type: Development Computer 
•	Connectivity: TCP/IP 
•	Port: 3306 
•	Open Windows Firewall port: normally leave enabled 
Click Next.
5. Create your MySQL password
You will see Authentication Method.
For a modern installation, use:
Use Strong Password Encryption
Then create a password for the MySQL root user.
For example:
MySQL@12345
Remember this password. You'll need it when connecting through Python, Django, MySQL Workbench, etc.
Click Next.
6. Windows Service
Keep the default settings:
Configure MySQL Server as a Windows Service
Service Name: MySQL80
Start the MySQL Server at System Startup
Click Next → Execute.
Wait until the configuration finishes.
7. Open MySQL Workbench
After installation, open Windows Start and search:
MySQL Workbench
Open it.
You should see something similar to:
MySQL Connections

Local instance MySQL80
Click it and enter the password you created.

8. Test MySQL
Inside MySQL Workbench, open a new SQL tab and run:
CREATE DATABASE testdb;
Then:
SHOW DATABASES;
You should see:
information_schema
mysql
performance_schema
sys
testdb
That means MySQL is installed and working.
9. Check from Windows CMD
Open Command Prompt and type:
mysql --version
You should get something like:
mysql  Ver 8.0.xx for Win64
If Windows says:
'mysql' is not recognized...

